1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Listen to a talk in a psychology class.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:03,804
Professor:

3
00:00:03,804 --> 00:00:08,745
The bystander effect is a social psychological theory according to which individuals are less

4
00:00:08,745 --> 00:00:12,120
likely to offer help to a victim when other people are present.

5
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:18,040
The theory was first proposed by researchers in the 1960s, based on observations.

6
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:22,223
The bystander effect can be explained through two primary factors:

7
00:00:22,223 --> 00:00:25,260
diffusion of responsibility and social influence.

8
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:31,046
Diffusion of responsibility occurs when people believe that someone else will take action,

9
00:00:31,046 --> 00:00:34,060
and so they feel less personal responsibility.

10
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:41,210
Social influence involves looking to others for cues on how to behave, especially in ambiguous situations.

11
00:00:41,210 --> 00:00:47,260
If others are not helping, individuals may interpret the situation as non-emergency

12
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:49,150
and refrain from intervening.

13
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:54,952
More recent studies, however, have disputed the claim that the presence of more bystanders

14
00:00:54,952 --> 00:00:57,180
can decrease the likelihood of assistance.

15
00:00:57,180 --> 00:01:02,677
One study from 2019 that analyzed video footage from multiple cities found that in

16
00:01:02,677 --> 00:01:07,110
9 out of 10 incidents, at least one bystander did take action to help.

17
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:13,180
This study found that a bystander was actually more likely to intervene when more bystanders were present.

18
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:18,322
Some factors that may influence bystander behavior include the level of danger

19
00:01:18,322 --> 00:01:20,130
and whether bystanders know each other.
